<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Place Ducale</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1606–1628</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Place Ducale is the central square of Charleville, now Charleville-Mézières in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It was built between 1606 and about 1628 for Charles of Gonzaga, Duke of Nevers, as the heart of his new capital, with uniform arcaded houses of brick and stone on the model of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/place-des-vosges">Place Royale</a>, now the Place des <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/place-ducale.jpg" alt="Place Ducale" width="320" height="134" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Velvet · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charleville_place_ducale_face.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
